  "reading": {
    "format": "latex",
    "markdown": "# Chapter 7 · Relative Clauses\n\nA relative clause denotes an `⟨e,t⟩` predicate and joins its head noun by PM\n(§7.2).\n\n## 7.3 Variable binding at LF\n\nThree assumptions (§7.3): traces and pronouns are variables, interpreted\nrelative to an assignment; syntax supplies the co-indexation; a binder node\ntriggers abstraction. Traces: `tᵢ` denotes `xᵢ` (§7.3.1). **PA**: a node\n`[i β]` denotes the function taking `x` to `⟦β⟧` with `xᵢ` set to `x`.\n\nEngine note: the LP node in the trees is the book's λᵢ binder; *who/which* are\nvacuous (`λX . X`) and *that* is `λp . p`, so the abstract passes up unchanged.\n\n## 7.3.1 Relative clauses\n\nSubject gaps (Group A): the trace sits in subject position, so the movement is\nstring-vacuous, and PA turns the clause into the predicate the noun needs.\n\nObject gaps (Group B): the subject composes with the verb first and the trace\nfills the object slot; PA over the index yields the predicate, and PM conjoins\nit with the head noun.\n\nFull sentences (Group C): with *the*, the description presupposes a unique\nsatisfier of noun-plus-clause; compose clause → PM → article → sentence.\n"
  }
